  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Trump honours Microsoft's CEO after Vance drops H-1B visa hammer on company",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/trump-honours-microsoft-s-ceo-after-vance-drops-h-1b-visa-hammer-on-company-126100900028_1.html",
      "description": "President Donald Trump celebrated tech billionaires such as Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella on Thursday, only to get upstaged hours earlier when Vice President JD Vance announced that Microsoft was being suspended from a programme that allows companies to apply for green cards for skilled foreign workers with H-1B visas.\nThe crackdown on Microsoft came under Vance's purview as the head of a fraud task force, but the targeting revealed the tension between the companies powering US economic growth and the administration's efforts to reduce immigration that it says has come at the expense of American workers.\n\"Our message to Microsoft is: You're a great American company, but you've got to hire great American workers,\" Vance told a news conference. He said the company had abused the visa system. \"You cannot lay off American workers and then replace them with foreign indentured servants.\"\nVance said Microsoft last year fired 6,000 US workers but also obtained 6,300 H-1B visas and almost 3,00",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-09T02:33:01"
    },
    {
      "title": "80% of H-1B filings were for existing employees, not new hires: Microsoft",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/80-of-h-1b-filings-were-for-existing-employees-not-new-hires-microsoft-126100900048_1.html",
      "description": "Microsoft has said 80 per cent of the 6000 H1-B visa applications it had submitted last fiscal year were to extend or change the status of existing employees and not to hire new people.\nThe statement came on Thursday hours after Vice President J D Vance suspended Microsoft from a programme to apply for green cards for foreign workers, accusing it of abusing the system.\n\"We believe in the strength and talent of the American workforce. That is why the vast majority of Microsoft employees in the US are Americans. We look forward to providing the administration with additional information,\" Microsoft said.\n\"For example, of the approximately 6,000 H-1B visa applications we submitted in the last fiscal year, 80 per cent were to extend or change the status of existing Microsoft employees. These were not to hire new people,\" it said.\nThe remaining filings for new employees were for individuals already legally in the United States who decided to come work for us, and they equal only one per",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-09T02:31:35"
    },
    {
      "title": "Trump Administration Cuts Off Access to a Major Immigration Program for Workers at Microsoft and Adobe",
      "link": "https://www.wired.com/story/trump-administration-crackdown-microsoft-adobe-immigration/",
      "description": "“There has been no company in the United States, unfortunately, that has abused this system more than Microsoft,” Vice President JD Vance said on Thursday.",
      "source_name": "Wired Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-08T19:11:45"
    }
